## The idea

Picture the sums as a grid: row i is a[i] + b[0], a[i] + b[1], … Each row is sorted, so the next smallest pair is always the **front of some row**. Keep one "next" pair per row in a **min-heap**; pop the smallest, then push the next pair from the same row.

## Step by step

1. Push (i, 0) for each row i < k, with priority (sum, i, j).
2. Pop the smallest; add (a[i], b[j]) to the answer.
3. If j + 1 < |b|, push (i, j + 1).
4. Stop after k pops (or when the heap is empty).
5. Variation: the same loop, keeping only the last sum.

## Worked example

**Input:** a = [1, 7, 11], b = [2, 4, 6], k = 3

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| seed | (0,0)=3, (1,0)=9, (2,0)=13 |
| pop 3 | take (1,2); push (0,1)=5 |
| pop 5 | take (1,4); push (0,2)=7 |
| pop 7 | take (1,6) |

**Result:** [(1,2), (1,4), (1,6)]; the 3rd smallest sum is 7

## Cost

O(k log min(k, |a|)) time, O(min(k, |a|)) memory. Sums are long, so no overflow.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LinkedIn (reported Oct 2026, "variation") — LeetCode 373 Find K Pairs with Smallest Sums.
public static class SmallestPairs {
    // a and b are sorted ascending. Return the k pairs (a[i], b[j]) with the smallest sums, in order of sum;
    // ties by i, then j. Fewer than k pairs exist → all of them.
    public static List<(int, int)> KSmallest(int[] a, int[] b, int k) {
        var result = new List<(int, int)>();
        if (a == null || b == null || a.Length == 0 || b.Length == 0 || k <= 0) return result;
        // The heap holds at most one candidate per row i: (i, j) where j is the next column to try in that row.
        // Priority = (sum, i, j) so ties come out in a fixed order.
        var heap = new PriorityQueue<(int i, int j), (long sum, int i, int j)>();
        for (int i = 0; i < a.Length && i < k; i++) heap.Enqueue((i, 0), ((long)a[i] + b[0], i, 0));
        while (heap.Count > 0 && result.Count < k) {
            var (i, j) = heap.Dequeue();
            result.Add((a[i], b[j]));
            if (j + 1 < b.Length) heap.Enqueue((i, j + 1), ((long)a[i] + b[j + 1], i, j + 1));   // the next pair in this row
        }
        return result;
    }

    // The variation: only the k-th smallest SUM (k is 1-based). Same heap, no list.
    public static long KthSmallestSum(int[] a, int[] b, int k) {
        var heap = new PriorityQueue<(int i, int j), (long sum, int i, int j)>();
        for (int i = 0; i < a.Length && i < k; i++) heap.Enqueue((i, 0), ((long)a[i] + b[0], i, 0));
        long sum = 0;
        for (int taken = 0; taken < k && heap.Count > 0; taken++) {
            var (i, j) = heap.Dequeue();
            sum = (long)a[i] + b[j];
            if (j + 1 < b.Length) heap.Enqueue((i, j + 1), ((long)a[i] + b[j + 1], i, j + 1));
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why one candidate per row?**

Rows are sorted, so a row's next pair can only become useful after its current one is taken.

**Q: Overflow?**

Two ints can sum past int.MaxValue — use long.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(k log min(k, |a|)) time, O(min(k, |a|)) heap.

**Q: k very large?**

Binary search on the sum value and count pairs ≤ mid with two pointers: O((|a| + |b|) log range).

**Q: Shared state?**

None.

**Q: Arrays on two machines.**

Stream the smallest prefix of each (you never need more than k from either).

